# 0076-shader-preparation-all-shaders-low-priority-background.patch -- the main app compiles ALL the built games' shaders by itself, one game after another (a persisted queue), at LOW priority
# (Utility QoS, at most two workers by default, one in the background, none when the phone is hot), in the foreground and under the continued-processing task in the background, with a Pause /
# Resume kept across launches and a Settings row for the cores. Exported players keep their first start as it is (the seed list, capped at 4,000, 4 workers at UserInitiated).
# Applies after 0075. UNTESTED ON DEVICE AND IN THE SIMULATOR: compiled only, and its pure part ran in a host check (below). Nothing here is measured on an iPhone yet.
#
# WHY (the owner; field facts, iPhone 15 Pro, Ocarina of Time (Switch 2), 42,848 libraries, logs of 2026-11-20)
#   * 0070's automatic run (the seed list, 4 workers at UserInitiated) ran 409 s right before Play; the game then started at thermal Serious, CPU ~1.15 GHz (2.5-3.1 GHz in an earlier session), 5-10 fps.
#   * After one killed game session every later launch logged "shader warm-up: not resumed at this launch (gameapp.boot-only: ...)" while the app said "gameapp.boot-only present, but a boot from it
#     was started and never came back — not starting again": 0070 treated an ATTEMPTED boot-only request (first word "attempted", GameAppUi.ClassifyBootRequest) like an armed one, so the
#     preparation never resumed. An attempted request starts nothing at a launch (the owner taps Play), so it no longer blocks the resume; only an ARMED one, gameapp.args or gameapp.continue do.
#   * iOS's Metal compile cache kept about 5,500-5,800 of this game's 42,848 libraries in earlier sessions. The owner wants them ALL compiled anyway; the run logs once, when it passes 5,500 libraries,
#     that iOS was seen to keep only ~5,500-5,800 of them, so the next game log can be read against it (hits are 1-4 ms, a compile again 200-1,800 ms).
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#   * Order and scope (GameWarmLogic.WarmKind.Everything, TakeCount): the seed list (pso_manifest.tsv's keys, in the order the host asks for them, then pso_user.tsv's, then the used keys), then the
#     learned libraries, then every other library of the pack; no cap. warmed.tsv rows are still skipped. A completed game writes prepared.tsv with an "all<TAB><count><TAB><time>" row
#     (PreparedText); only that row ends it (PreparedDone(all: true)): 0070's capped marker of the same app build does not. The exported player's seed-list run and the manual "whole library" run of a
#     player are unchanged (WarmKind.SeedList, WarmKind.Library).
#   * Workers (GameWarmLogic.AutoWorkers(choice, thermal, background, processors), read by every worker before every library, so a change applies to the run that is going): thermal Serious or
#     Critical -> 0 (the workers wait and look again every 2-3 s); in the background -> 1, whatever the setting; foreground, Auto -> 2 at Nominal, 1 at Fair; foreground, a fixed N -> N below Serious
#     (Fair does not cut it, the owner's choice), clamped to MaxCores = processors - 2 (1..4). The run starts MaxCores threads; those numbered at or above the allowed count wait 2 s and look again
#     (so a lower count lets the others finish their current library and stop; a higher one starts them within 2 s). The memory wait stays (below 700 MiB available). The "rest only on a charger"
#     rule does not apply to this run. Low Power Mode does NOT pause it (the owner's correction): it is logged once when the run starts ("iOS runs the CPU slower, the preparation goes on").
#   * QoS: Utility in the foreground, Background in the background, set by each worker on itself with pthread_set_qos_class_self_np (QOS_CLASS_UTILITY 0x11 / QOS_CLASS_BACKGROUND 0x09, libc) when
#     the app's state changes (GameWarm.InBackground, set from DidEnterBackground / WillEnterForeground). A failed call is logged once.
#   * Settings › Build › "Shader preparation" (GameAppShelf.cs BuildSettingsShelf, GameAppWarm.cs ChooseShaderCores / SetShaderCores): Auto (low priority) (default), 1 core … N cores up to
#     MaxCores; stored as shader_prep_cores in Documents/gameapp.settings.tsv ("auto" or the count; anything unreadable = Auto); the note under it is the owner's text; a change is logged
#     ("shader warm-up: cores set to …") and applied to the run in progress (GameWarm.CoresChoice).
#   * The queue (Documents/gameapp.warmqueue.tsv, "# shader preparation queue v1", rows "paused<TAB>0|1" and "title<TAB><id>"; written to .part then renamed; unreadable or foreign = rebuilt from
#     disk): WarmSync reads, on a worker, which built games (GameStatus.Ready: the fresh framework or a parked build of 0073; packs are Documents/shaders/<id> for every game) owe a preparation,
#     and SyncQueue drops the ones that owe none (prepared, deleted, pack gone), appends the owing ones in the shelf's order and puts `front` first (a game whose build just FINISHED, or the owner's
#     "Prepare now"). WarmKick starts NextToRun: the first title, unless the queue is paused or something else has the phone (WarmBusy: a build or boot, an export or its sizing, a delete or its
#     sizing, a download, the build queue). A completed game leaves the queue (GameWarm.Ended) and the next starts by itself. A run whose game left the queue is stopped.
#   * When it starts: at a launch (StartWarmAtLaunch: queue loaded at once; 3 s later WarmSync + the 10 s kicker) unless this launch builds or boots (LaunchBlocker: gameapp.args, gameapp.continue, an
#     ARMED boot-only; held until that run ended or the owner came back to the app); after every build, whatever its outcome (PrepareAfterBuild, the existing build-end hook); 2 s after
#     WillEnterForeground; after a delete; every 10 s when the phone is free again (an export or a download that ended). Play, a build or a boot (BeginRun, unchanged), an export (StartExport) and
#     a delete (PerformDelete) stop it; what is done is kept in warmed.tsv.
#   * Pause / Resume (ToggleWarmPause, kept in the queue file across launches; pausing stops the run and ends its continued-processing task): a button under the bar in the game's sheet and in the
#     Build tab's new SHADERS section (line, bar, Pause / Resume, a note; the look of the build queue's section above it), and "Pause / Resume the shader preparation" in the Library tile's and row's
#     long-press menu (a tile or row is one big button, so its strip has no button of its own). "Prepare now" (the sheet's SHADERS row, which was "Prepare the shaders…" in the main app; the game
#     settings' button reaches the same PrepareShadersFor) moves the game to the front, resumes the queue and stops another game's run; on the game being prepared it pauses. The long-press menu
#     of a queued game has "Prepare the shaders now".
#   * Lines: tile, row, sheet and Build tab "Preparing the shaders · Ocarina of Time · 1,200 of 42,848 · 2 more games waiting" (QueueLine; " · paused while the phone is hot" / " · waiting for memory"
#     when no worker compiles), "Shader preparation paused · <game> · N more games waiting" when paused (PausedLine). 0070's redraw throttling (one main-thread post in flight, 500 ms Coalescer)
#     and in-place bar updates are kept; nothing new walks a folder on the main thread (the packs and markers are read on a worker in WarmSync).
#   * Background (GameAppBackground.cs): the automatic run asks for the continued-processing task when it starts in the foreground; started in the background (after a build that ended there) it is
#     asked for at the next DidBecomeActive (EnsureWarmTask), and so is a task iOS refused or expired (the warm-up's expiration now completes the task and clears it, instead of leaving its id in
#     flight, which made every later BackgroundBegin return). Its title is "Preparing shaders · 1,200 of 42,848" (ActivityTitle), its subtitle the game (and why it waits); the bar is the fraction.
#     When the queue moves to the next game while the task is still in flight, the same task carries on (BackgroundRetargetWarm: the game's name, the bar from 0). It ends at completion, at a
#     pause, and when a build, a boot, an export or a delete starts.
#   * GameAppMain.cs: the launch hook's and the build-end hook's comments, StopWarm at the start of StartExport and PerformDelete, WarmSync at the end of a delete. Nothing else there.
#
# VERIFIED (2026-11-20, M1 Pro)
#   * Compile: a copy of the generated device project (gameapp.csproj, sources re-pointed to the copy, GameAppSlot.cs and GameAppSlotUi.cs added, the project's own GameAppExport.cs and
#     GameAppBuild.cs kept), `dotnet build gameapp.csproj -c Release -r ios-arm64 -t:Compile -nologo`: the 0075 tree rc 0, 0 errors, 38 warnings; with this patch rc 0, 0 errors, 40 warnings, the
#     same 27 distinct warnings (the 2 more lines are an existing CA1416 on BGContinuedProcessingTask.Progress, in BackgroundRetargetWarm). No .ipa was made.
#   * Host check of the pure part (GameAppWarm.cs compiled with WARM_LOGIC_ONLY, net11.0): 73 of 73 checks (TakeCount for the three kinds, the order of the run of every library on a synthetic
#     8,002-library pack, AutoWorkers over thermal / background / setting / core count, ParseCores / CoresLabel / CoresValue, PauseReason, PreparedDone against 0070's marker, LaunchBlocker,
#     the queue's parse / format / sync / next / remove / waiting, the lines). 12 of 12 mutations killed (the cap on the run of every library, two workers in the background, no pause at Serious,
#     Fair cutting a fixed count, an unclamped count, the front put last, prepared games kept queued, the pause ignored, any boot-only blocking, 0070's marker ending the run, the paused flag
#     not read, the waiting games dropped from the line).
#
# NOT VERIFIED / KNOWN EDGES
#   * Nothing ran on an iPhone or in the simulator: the worker counts, the QoS calls (pthread_set_qos_class_self_np on .NET threads), the time a full run takes (42,848 libraries; the 409 s of 0070
#     were ~4,000), the heat it leaves before a game, whether iOS accepts the continued-processing task for a run this long, and whether iOS keeps more than ~5,800 libraries in its cache are all to
#     be measured. Background QoS under a continued-processing task may be very slow.
#   * The attempted-vs-armed decision uses GameAppUi.ClassifyBootRequest on the device; the host check covers LaunchBlocker only (the classification is GameAppUi's, not compiled in the check).
#   * An armed boot-only request that the launch then withdraws (a stale framework, a distribution certificate) keeps the queue held until the owner comes back to the app or a build ends.
#   * A build that ends while iBuilder is in the background: the queue's run starts there without a task (iOS accepts it only in the foreground) and goes on while iOS lets the process run;
#     the task is asked for at the next DidBecomeActive.
#   * The Dynamic Island's stop button expires the task; for the preparation that is not told apart from a system expiration: the run goes on and the task is asked for again at the next
#     foreground. Pause is the way to stop it.
#   * Resetting the task's bar to 0 when the queue moves to the next game goes backwards; whether iOS treats that as a stall is not known.
#   * The game settings' button (GameAppSettingsViewController, GameAppMain.cs) still reads "Stop preparing the shaders" while any game is being prepared; for another game a tap is "Prepare now".
#   * While a run goes, the build cleanup offer (GameAppBuildCleanupUi.cs) is still not made (it counts GameWarm.Active as busy); with a run of every library that is most of the time.
#   * A plain download blocks the start (WarmBusy counts every download: a download-and-build cannot be told apart from it) but does not stop a run that is going.
